Awesome GPT Image 2.5 Prompts -- what you may reuse, and what belongs to
someone else.

The build scripts are open source.

    scripts/**    MIT    Copyright (c) 2026 Formative Intelligence Inc.
                         Full text: LICENSES/MIT.txt

Fork them, build on them, ship them commercially. Credit us and our part is
done. They are the reusable piece here: no dependencies, no network, no
secrets, and they will rebuild this whole repository from data/prompts.json.

The prompts are not ours to give you.

    The 150 prompt bodies and their titles, wherever they appear -- in
    data/prompts.json, in prompts/*.md, and quoted in any README.

Each was written by the person credited on its row and first published in the
post linked beside it. ATTRIBUTION.md records who, where, and which collection
we received it through: 115 via one that stated CC0 1.0, 35 via one that stated
CC BY 4.0. Copies of what each collection said, at the commit we took them
from, are in provenance/.

We have not independently established the chain of rights behind each prompt,
and we make no representation to you about it. If you want to build on one for
something that matters, the credit line tells you whose work it is and where to
find them. If you wrote one and want it gone, TAKEDOWN.md -- it goes, no
explanation needed.

Everything else we assembled -- the selection, the nine use cases, the
ordering, the prose, the translations -- is

    Copyright (c) 2026 Formative Intelligence Inc.

and we are not offering it under an open licence at this time. Not to be
awkward: some of that copy came to us from elsewhere in the company and we have
not traced its origin, so granting you rights in it would mean claiming rights
we have not verified we hold. That is the mistake this file exists to avoid.
Ask us if you want to reuse a piece of it.

GitHub reads this file, finds no single SPDX identifier, and labels the
repository "Other". REUSE.toml has the machine-readable map.
